.68433,"lat":25.37877,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q9050155","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nike_Missile_Site_HM-69","tour":5,"summary":{"extract":"The Nike Missile Site HM-69 is a former Nike-Hercules missile base, now listed as a historic site west of Homestead, Florida, United States. It is located on Long Pine Key Road in the Everglades National Park. The site with 22 buildings opened in 1964 and closed in 1979 when it was turned over to the National Park Service.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nike_Missile_Site_HM-69","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/11042430362","name":"Chevelier","kind":"historic","lon":-81.21666,"lat":25.72035,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q28209358","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chevelier,_Florida","tour":2,"summary":{"extract":"Chevelier was a planned community located near Chevelier Point on Chevelier Bay, currently in the Everglades National Park. It is in Monroe County, Florida, United States.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chevelier%2C_Florida","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/11042430363","name":"Poinciana","kind":"historic","lon":-81.13342,"lat":25.61151,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q112150996","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poinciana,_Monroe_County,_Florida","tour":3,"summary":{"extract":"Poinciana was a planned community that was to be located along the Lostmans River in Monroe County, Florida. Poinciana has been described as the Everglades's \"most spectacular commercial enterprise\" and \"the prototype of all Florida scams\".","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poinciana%2C_Monroe_County%2C_Florida","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/154031039","name":"Chokoloskee","kind":"place","lon":-81.35941,"lat":25.8144,"ele_m":3.0,"wikidata":"Q1075868","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chokoloskee,_Florida","tour":1,"summary":{"extract":"Chokoloskee is an unincorporated community and census-designated place (CDP) located at the edge of the Ten Thousand Islands in Collier County, Florida, United States. The population was 345 at the 2020 census, down from 359 at the 2010 census. It is part of the Naples–Marco Island Metropolitan Statistical Area.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chokoloskee%2C_Florida","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Seminole_chickee_on_Chokoloskee_Island%2C_Florida_%289492237347%29.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seminole_chickee_on_Chokoloskee_Island,_Florida_(9492237347).jpg","dist_m":175,"width":640,"height":454,"license":"Public domain","license_url":"","artist":"State Library and Archives of Florida"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Smallwood_store.JPG/960px-Smallwood_store.JPG","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smallwood_store.JPG","dist_m":357,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Palmtreeshadows"}],"street":{"image_id":"859458417943543","username":"compassdata","captured_at":"2017-09-18","is_pano":true,"dist_m":6,"url":"https://www.mapillary.com/app/?pKey=859458417943543","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}},{"id":"node/154353700","name":"Flamingo","kind":"place","lon":-80.92526,"lat":25.14189,"ele_m":1.0,"wikidata":"Q735555","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flamingo,_Monroe_County,_Florida","tour":6,"summary":{"extract":"Flamingo is the southernmost headquarters of Everglades National Park, in Monroe County, Florida, United States. Flamingo is one of the two end points of the 99-mile (159-km) Wilderness Waterway, and the southern end of the only road through the park from Florida City. It began as a small coastal settlement on the eastern end of Cape Sable on the southern tip of the Florida peninsula, facing Florida Bay. The actual town of Flamingo was located approximately 4+1⁄2 miles west of the current Flamingo campground area. All that remains of the former town are a few remnants of building foundations, and it is considered a ghost town.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flamingo%2C_Florida","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Tillandsia_usneoides_kz02.jpg/960px-Tillandsia_usneoides_kz02.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tillandsia_usneoides_kz02.jpg","dist_m":82,"width":640,"height":850,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Tillandsia_usneoides_kz07.jpg/960px-Tillandsia_usneoides_kz07.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tillandsia_usneoides_kz07.jpg","dist_m":82,"width":640,"height":850,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Tillandsia_usneoides_kz11.jpg/960px-Tillandsia_usneoides_kz11.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tillandsia_usneoides_kz11.jpg","dist_m":83,"width":640,"height":482,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Tillandsia_usneoides_kz10.jpg/960px-Tillandsia_usneoides_kz10.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tillandsia_usneoides_kz10.jpg","dist_m":83,"width":640,"height":482,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Flamingo_ranger_station.jpg/960px-Flamingo_ranger_station.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flamingo_ranger_station.jpg","dist_m":87,"width":640,"height":427,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Daniel Schwen"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Tillandsia_usneoides_kz13.jpg/960px-Tillandsia_usneoides_kz13.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tillandsia_usneoides_kz13.jpg","dist_m":96,"width":640,"height":482,"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","artist":"Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz"}],"street":{"image_id":"266280435234485","username":"thetornado76","captured_at":"2018-05-18","is_pano":false,"dist_m":43,"url":"https://www.mapillary.com/app/?pKey=266280435234485","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}},{"id":"node/1576354674","name":"Shark Valley","kind":"place","lon":-80.76348,"lat":25.74014,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q3481503","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark_Valley","tour":4,"summary":{"extract":"Shark Valley is a geological depression at the head of the Shark River Slough in far western Miami-Dade County, Florida, United States. It is currently part of Everglades National Park. Shark Valley empties into Shark River in the Ten Thousand Islands of Monroe County. Shark Valley characteristically includes sawgrass prairie that floods during the rainy season, hence the name \"river of grass\"—Pa-Hay-Okee, from the Mikasuki language—for such marshes in the Everglades. Shark Valley features a Visitor Center with educational displays, a park video, an underwater camera and informational brochures. The entrance to Shark Valley is located along Tamiami Trail near the Miami-Dade–Collier County line.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark_Valley","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/The_West_road_in_Shark_Valley%5E_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-The_West_road_in_Shark_Valley%5E_-_panoramio.jpg","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_West_road_in_Shark_Valley%5E_-_panoramio.jpg","dist_m":354,"width":640,"height":480,"license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","artist":"paulsmithrj"}],"street":null},{"id":"way/49368009","name":"Tavernier","kind":"place","lon":-80.52248,"lat":25.02443,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q2143708","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tavernier,_Florida","tour":7,"summary":{"extract":"Tavernier is a census-designated place (CDP) and unincorporated community in Monroe County, Florida, United States on Key Largo, the largest island in the upper Florida Keys. Tavernier's population was 2,530 at the 2020 census, up from 2,136 in 2010.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tavernier%2C_Florida","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Wikipedia"},"photos":[],"street":null},{"id":"node/4359216900","name":"Miccosukee Indian Village","kind":"place","lon":-80.77479,"lat":25.75971,"ele_m":null,"wikidata":"Q5306051","url":null}],"tour":["node/5477084519","node/154031039","node/11042430362","node/11042430363","node/1576354674","relation/20405698","node/154353700","way/49368009"],"tour_source":"auto","missing_stops":[]}